% The solver core of the CHR type-checker (typechecker2).
%
% `tc2_walk.chr` walks the encoded AST and tells the `check_*`
% constraints below; everything here is the solving itself — the type
% representation, the meet table, overload resolution, bound discharge
% and the guard-derived-evidence rules.
%
% Three things about its interfaces, all load-bearing:
%
%   * the module name. The type representation below is what
%     diagnostics render, and `typeModule` in
%     `src/YCHR/Internal/TypeCheck/Render.hs` strips the
%     `'$tc2_solver'` qualifier off a type atom on the way out, so this
%     module may not be renamed without renaming that too;
%   * `Ctx` is the structured `ctx/4` of `'$tc2_diag'`, carrying a
%     diagnostic's checking unit, origin, label and location. The rules
%     pass it opaquely, so nothing below reads it;
%   * the diagnostic vocabulary — the accumulators, `error/3`,
%     `warning/3` and the code and detail types — lives in
%     `'$tc2_diag'`, which the declaration checks share, and is
%     declared rather than `any`: the detail pair is spelled `tpair`
%     there, so as not to collide with `library(pairs)`, and a
%     detail that is a bare name travels wrapped in `mangled_name`.
%
% Two renames are owed to this module and not yet made:
%
%   * split `sig_t/2` (and `sig_fst`/`sig_snd`) into two types. One
%     carries a function signature and should read `args`/`ret`; the
%     other carries a constructor signature and should read
%     `parent`/`fields`. Sharing one polymorphic pair makes both call
%     sites say less than they could, and `sig_fst` means something
%     different depending on which one it is applied to;
%   * spell out the matcher's `mok` / `mfail` / `mp` — the `msub`
%     result and its substitution pairs.
:- module('$tc2_solver', [
    type(ty/0),
    type(sig_t/2),
    type(ev_depth/0),
    type(bound_named/0),
    type(bcand/0),
    constraint_sig/3,
    function_sig/3,
    function_sigs/3,
    function_bounds/3,
    constraint_bounds/3,
    con_sig/2,
    check_constraint_use/5,
    check_function_use/6,
    check_function_use_with_ambient/7,
    check_function_ref/7,
    check_constructor_use/4,
    check_unify/3,
    check_guard_bool/2,
    check_guard_match/5,
    check_guard_getarg/5,
    ev_unify/4,
    eq_unify/4,
    rigid_supply/1,
    check_bound/6
]).

:- use_module('$tc2_diag').
:- use_module(library(prelude)).
:- use_module(library(lists)).

% Type-representation algebraic type. `encode_ty` in `tc2_walk.chr`
% builds these out of a source `ty_expr`: base types are 0-arity
% constructors, type constructors and function types are compound
% terms.
%
% The base-type constructors carry a `ty_` prefix because a data
% constructor may not share a bare name with a visible function
% (YCHR-20020) and the prelude declares `float/1` and `string/1`. The
% prefix is internal: `displayTypeAtom` in
% `src/YCHR/Internal/TypeCheck/Render.hs` strips it again, so
% user-facing messages still read `int`, `float`, `string`,
% `any`. All four are prefixed, not just the two that clash, so the
% strip is one uniform rule.
%
% The first field of `tcon` is `any` because a type
% constructor name may be either an atom (`bool`) or a qualified
% atom (e.g. `prelude:bool`). Qualified type names that originate in
% this CHR source — as opposed to arriving inside an encoded program —
% must be wrapped in `quote/1` so the renamer treats them as
% opaque data rather than as cross-module references subject to
% value-level visibility checks. See language.md §The `quote/1`
% quoting form.
% `rigid(Cell, N)` is a *rigid* type variable: a fresh identity `N`
% allocated by the walker while checking a polymorphic declaration's
% own equations or rule bodies. Unlike unbound CHR variables (which
% are *flexible* and consistent with every declared type per the
% gradual guarantee), a rigid tvar is only consistent with itself and
% `any`. This is what closes the soundness gap where a polymorphic
% body calling an overloaded function at its own type parameter would
% silently type-check without a `requiring` clause.
%
% `Cell` is the skolem's *pin slot*: a fresh unbound variable that
% guard-derived evidence — and nothing else — may bind to a type
% (docs/reference/type-system.md §Guard-Derived Type Evidence §What
% evidence does). While the cell is unbound the tvar is *unpinned*
% and behaves as an opaque constant; once evidence pins it, every
% consumer below dereferences through the cell and the tvar behaves
% as the pinned type. One binding reaches every occurrence of the
% skolem because all occurrences share the one term.
%
% INVARIANT: never `copy_term` a structure containing a live unpinned
% cell — a copy would get a *different* cell and silently stop
% tracking the pin. This holds today because the only structures the
% rules copy are declared signatures (whose tvars are flexible, never
% rigid) and constructor signatures; ambient signatures, which do
% carry the enclosing declaration's rigids, are deliberately applied
% in place (see the `cand` type's `amb` arm).
:- chr_type ty ---> ty_int
                  ; ty_float
                  ; ty_string
                  ; ty_any
                  ; tcon(any, list(ty))
                  ; fun(list(ty), ty)
                  ; rigid(ty, int).

% Polymorphic two-field record reused for both function signatures
% (sig_t(list(ty), ty)) and constructor signatures
% (sig_t(ty, list(ty))).
:- chr_type sig_t(A, B) ---> sig(A, B).

% The diagnostic vocabulary — `error/3`, `warning/3`, the `error_code`
% and `warning_code` atoms and the `error_detail` payloads — is
% declared in `'$tc2_diag'`, together with the accumulators
% `report_error/3` and `report_warning/3` that the rules below emit
% into. It is shared with the declaration checks of `tc2_pure.chr`,
% which produce the same `error/3` terms without going through a
% solver at all. Both accumulators are declared over those types, so
% `ychr check --Werror` rejects a report site here that invents a
% code — but not one that hands a code the wrong detail; see the
% `error/3` declaration there for exactly what the typing does and
% does not catch, and why the names below are wrapped.
%
% `bound_unsatisfied` is emitted from `check_bound` when no declared
% signature of the bound's named function is consistent with the
% substituted bound. The other bounded-polymorphism error codes
% (unbound bound variable, unknown bound function, bound cycle,
% extend-on-bounded) are produced by the Haskell resolver and never
% flow through this CHR program.

% Depth marker threaded through `eq_unify` / `ev_unify`. `ev_top`
% relates two whole types; `ev_param` marks the parameter positions
% reached by descending into a shared type constructor. The
% distinction is load-bearing for rigid variables: value equality
% determines a type's outermost constructor nominally, but a
% parameter-erasing value (`[]`, `empty`) leaves the parameters
% undetermined, so at `ev_param` equality evidence derives nothing
% about rigid cells — no pins, no merges, no contradiction warnings
% (docs/reference/type-system.md §What evidence does).
:- chr_type ev_depth ---> ev_top ; ev_param.

% A named bound signature inside a `requiring` clause: the bound
% function's flat-atom name together with the argument-types and
% return-type of its required signature. The fields share logical
% variables with the enclosing declaration's primary signature; both
% are bundled in `function_bounds` / `constraint_bounds` so a single
% `copy_term` freshens them consistently at each use site. Storing
% the args/ret flat (rather than wrapped in `sig_t`) keeps the type
% checker's pattern inference simple, and matches the shape the
% discharge rule consumes.
:- chr_type bound_named ---> nbound(any, list(ty), ty).

% A resolution candidate, tagged with its provenance. Ambient
% signatures (contributed by an enclosing `requiring` clause) are
% applied in place — their type variables deliberately share identity
% with the enclosing declaration's — while declared signatures are
% freshly renamed (`copy_term`) at commit time so no use site can
% pollute the stored declaration. A bounded declared signature
% additionally carries its bounds (sharing variables with the
% signature, copied together at commit) and the candidate signature
% lists for each bound's named function.
:- chr_type cand
    ---> amb(sig_t(list(ty), ty))
       ; decl(sig_t(list(ty), ty))
       ; decl_bounded(sig_t(list(ty), ty), list(bound_named), list(bcand)).

% Candidate signatures for one bound's named function, assembled by
% `tc2_walk.chr` at emission time (the discharge rules are pure and
% cannot gather across the store). Ambient and declared candidates
% are kept apart because they discharge differently: a declared
% signature's variables are per-emission fresh and never bound, so a
% consistent declared witness is stable; an ambient signature may
% share still-flexible variables with the enclosing scope, so it can
% witness success only once its own leaves are solid.
:- chr_type bcand
    ---> bc(any, list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(sig_t(list(ty), ty))).

:- chr_constraint
    constraint_sig(any, int, list(ty)),
    function_sig(any, int, sig_t(list(ty), ty)),
    function_sigs(any, int, list(sig_t(list(ty), ty))),
    function_bounds(any, int, list(bound_named)),
    constraint_bounds(any, int, list(bound_named)),
    con_sig(any, sig_t(ty, list(ty))),
    check_constraint_use(any, int, list(bcand), list(ty), ctx),
    check_function_use(any, int, list(bcand), list(ty), ty, ctx),
    check_function_use_with_ambient(any, int, list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(bcand), list(ty), ty, ctx),
    check_function_ref(any, int, list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(bcand), list(ty), ty, ctx),
    resolve_funref(any, list(cand), list(ty), ty, ctx),
    check_constructor_use(any, list(ty), ty, ctx),
    check_unify(ty, ty, ctx),
    check_guard_bool(ty, ctx),
    check_guard_match(ty, any, list(ty), list(ty), ctx),
    check_guard_getarg(ty, ty, any, int, ctx),
    check_arg_list(list(ty), list(ty), ctx),
    tc_unify(ty, ty, ctx),
    tc_unify_list(list(ty), list(ty), ctx),
    ev_unify(ty, ty, ev_depth, ctx),
    ev_unify_list(list(ty), list(ty), ev_depth, ctx),
    eq_unify(ty, ty, ev_depth, ctx),
    eq_unify_list(list(ty), list(ty), ev_depth, ctx),
    rigid_supply(int),
    make_fresh_rigid(ty),
    make_fresh_rigids(list(ty), list(ty)),
    resolve_overload(any, list(cand), list(ty), ty, ctx),
    commit_candidate(list(cand), list(ty), ty, ctx),
    check_bound(any, list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(ty), ty, ctx),
    emit_bounds(list(bound_named), list(bcand), ctx).

:- function (sig_fst(sig_t(A, B)) -> A), (sig_snd(sig_t(A, B)) -> B).
sig_fst(sig(X, _)) -> X.
sig_snd(sig(_, Y)) -> Y.

:- function
    (all_nonvar_list(list(any)) -> bool),
    (cand_sig(cand) -> sig_t(list(ty), ty)),
    (wrap_ambs(list(sig_t(list(ty), ty))) -> list(cand)),
    (wrap_decls(list(sig_t(list(ty), ty))) -> list(cand)),
    (bcand_ambs(any, list(bcand)) -> list(sig_t(list(ty), ty))),
    (bcand_decls(any, list(bcand)) -> list(sig_t(list(ty), ty))),
    (filter_consistent(list(cand), list(ty)) -> list(cand)),
    (sig_args_consistent(list(ty), list(ty)) -> bool),
    (candidates_committable(list(cand)) -> bool),
    (cands_all_equal(sig_t(list(ty), ty), list(cand)) -> bool),
    (no_candidate_consistent(list(cand), list(ty)) -> bool).

all_nonvar_list([]) -> true.
all_nonvar_list([X|_]) | var(X) -> false.
all_nonvar_list([_|Xs]) -> all_nonvar_list(Xs).

cand_sig(amb(Sig)) -> Sig.
cand_sig(decl(Sig)) -> Sig.
cand_sig(decl_bounded(Sig, _, _)) -> Sig.

wrap_ambs([]) -> [].
wrap_ambs([Sig | Rest]) -> [amb(Sig) | wrap_ambs(Rest)].

wrap_decls([]) -> [].
wrap_decls([Sig | Rest]) -> [decl(Sig) | wrap_decls(Rest)].

bcand_ambs(_, []) -> [].
bcand_ambs(N, [bc(N, Ambs, _) | _]) -> Ambs.
bcand_ambs(N, [_ | Rest]) -> bcand_ambs(N, Rest).

bcand_decls(_, []) -> [].
bcand_decls(N, [bc(N, _, Decls) | _]) -> Decls.
bcand_decls(N, [_ | Rest]) -> bcand_decls(N, Rest).

filter_consistent([], _) -> [].
filter_consistent([C|Rest], ArgTypes)
    | sig_args_consistent(sig_fst(cand_sig(C)), ArgTypes) ->
    [C | filter_consistent(Rest, ArgTypes)].
filter_consistent([_|Rest], ArgTypes) -> filter_consistent(Rest, ArgTypes).

sig_args_consistent(DeclArgs, CallArgs) ->
    is_mok(match_ty_list(DeclArgs, CallArgs, mok([]))).

% A pending resolution fires only when every surviving candidate
% denotes the same signature after substitution (spec §Resolution:
% "equal after substitution" — ask-equality `==` is deref-based
% structural equality with same-unbound-variable identity, which is
% exactly that relation). In the common case exactly one candidate
% survives.
candidates_committable([]) -> false.
candidates_committable([C | Rest]) -> cands_all_equal(cand_sig(C), Rest).

cands_all_equal(_, []) -> true.
cands_all_equal(Sig, [C | Rest]) | Sig == cand_sig(C) -> cands_all_equal(Sig, Rest).
cands_all_equal(_, _) -> false.

no_candidate_consistent(Cands, ArgTypes) ->
    cand_list_empty(filter_consistent(Cands, ArgTypes)).

% Full-type filtering for function references: unlike a call's
% resolution, the reference's expected RETURN component participates
% (spec §Function references — a reference has no argument
% expressions, so the expected function type is all the checker has).
:- function (filter_consistent_full(list(cand), list(ty), ty) -> list(cand)).
filter_consistent_full([], _, _) -> [].
filter_consistent_full([C|Rest], ArgTypes, RetType)
    | sig_one_consistent(cand_sig(C), ArgTypes, RetType) ->
    [C | filter_consistent_full(Rest, ArgTypes, RetType)].
filter_consistent_full([_|Rest], ArgTypes, RetType) ->
    filter_consistent_full(Rest, ArgTypes, RetType).

:- function (cand_list_empty(list(cand)) -> bool).
cand_list_empty([]) -> true.
cand_list_empty(_) -> false.

% ==========================================================================
% Consistency matcher (pure, no var binding)
% ==========================================================================
%
% The consistency relation of spec §Consistency, extended with the
% type-variable rules of §Type variables and instantiation: deep
% structural consistency on type constructors and function types, the
% absorbing rules for `any`, and *unification discipline* for type
% variables — threaded through an explicit substitution rather than
% by binding, so the matcher never mutates solver state and is safe
% to call from guards.
%
% The substitution records, for a variable on either side, the term
% it first met (keyed by variable identity via `==`); a later
% occurrence of the same variable must be consistent with the
% recorded term. This is what rejects `g(A, A)` against
% `(int, string)` — per-position independence would accept it.
% Known approximation: the repeat check compares with
% `existential_consistent`, which does not thread the substitution
% further into the recorded term; nested shared variables are
% checked leniently (in the gradual, non-rejecting direction).

:- chr_type mpair ---> mp(ty, ty).
:- chr_type msub ---> mfail ; mok(list(mpair)).
:- chr_type mfound ---> not_found ; found(ty).

:- function
    (match_ty(ty, ty, msub) -> msub),
    (match_ty_list(list(ty), list(ty), msub) -> msub),
    (match_var(ty, ty, mfound, list(mpair)) -> msub),
    (lookup_msub(ty, list(mpair)) -> mfound),
    (is_mok(msub) -> bool).

match_ty(_, _, mfail) -> mfail.
match_ty(D, A, mok(S)) | var(D) -> match_var(D, A, lookup_msub(D, S), S).
match_ty(D, A, mok(S)) | var(A) -> match_var(A, D, lookup_msub(A, S), S).
match_ty(ty_any, _, S) -> S.
match_ty(_, ty_any, S) -> S.
match_ty(ty_int, ty_int, S) -> S.
match_ty(ty_float, ty_float, S) -> S.
match_ty(ty_string, ty_string, S) -> S.
match_ty(rigid(C, N), rigid(C, N), S) -> S.
match_ty(rigid(C, _), A, S) | nonvar(C) -> match_ty(C, A, S).
match_ty(D, rigid(C, _), S) | nonvar(C) -> match_ty(D, C, S).
match_ty(tcon(C, A1), tcon(C, A2), S) -> match_ty_list(A1, A2, S).
match_ty(fun(A1, R1), fun(A2, R2), S) -> match_ty(R1, R2, match_ty_list(A1, A2, S)).
match_ty(_, _, _) -> mfail.

match_ty_list(_, _, mfail) -> mfail.
match_ty_list([], [], S) -> S.
match_ty_list([X|Xs], [Y|Ys], S) -> match_ty_list(Xs, Ys, match_ty(X, Y, S)).
match_ty_list(_, _, _) -> mfail.

match_var(V, T, not_found, S) -> mok([mp(V, T) | S]).
match_var(_, T, found(T0), S) | existential_consistent(T0, T) -> mok(S).
match_var(_, _, _, _) -> mfail.

lookup_msub(_, []) -> not_found.
lookup_msub(V, [mp(K, T) | _]) | V == K -> found(T).
lookup_msub(V, [_ | Rest]) -> lookup_msub(V, Rest).

is_mok(mok(_)) -> true.
is_mok(mfail) -> false.

% ==========================================================================
% Bounded polymorphism helpers (pure functions, no var binding)
% ==========================================================================

% Existential consistency: deep, non-binding consistency between two
% type-structures. Unlike `tc_unify` this never binds any variable,
% so it is safe to call from `check_bound`'s discharge guard when we
% need to know "does any candidate signature exist that is consistent
% with the substituted bound?" without modifying the surrounding
% solver state.
:- function
    (existential_consistent(ty, ty) -> bool),
    (existential_consistent_list(list(ty), list(ty)) -> bool),
    (sig_one_consistent(sig_t(list(ty), ty), list(ty), ty) -> bool),
    (sig_existential(list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(ty), ty) -> bool).

existential_consistent(T, _) | var(T) -> true.
existential_consistent(_, T) | var(T) -> true.
existential_consistent(ty_any, _) -> true.
existential_consistent(_, ty_any) -> true.
existential_consistent(ty_int, ty_int) -> true.
existential_consistent(ty_float, ty_float) -> true.
existential_consistent(ty_string, ty_string) -> true.
% Same-rigid succeeds; different-rigid (rigid(_, N), rigid(_, M) with
% N≠M) falls through to the `(_, _) -> false` fallthrough below, so two
% distinct rigid identities are deliberately inconsistent. This is
% what makes `check_bound`'s existence check reject an ambient
% candidate that doesn't share the call site's rigid σ. A *pinned*
% rigid (evidence bound its cell) is consistent with whatever its cell
% holds.
existential_consistent(rigid(C, N), rigid(C, N)) -> true.
existential_consistent(rigid(C, _), T) | nonvar(C) -> existential_consistent(C, T).
existential_consistent(T, rigid(C, _)) | nonvar(C) -> existential_consistent(T, C).
existential_consistent(tcon(C, A1), tcon(C, A2)) -> existential_consistent_list(A1, A2).
existential_consistent(fun(A1, R1), fun(A2, R2))
    | existential_consistent_list(A1, A2) -> existential_consistent(R1, R2).
existential_consistent(_, _) -> false.

existential_consistent_list([], []) -> true.
existential_consistent_list([X|Xs], [Y|Ys])
    | existential_consistent(X, Y) -> existential_consistent_list(Xs, Ys).
existential_consistent_list(_, _) -> false.

% A single declared signature is consistent with the substituted
% bound when its argument list and return type match under one shared
% substitution (the matcher above): a declared signature with a
% repeated type variable, like `g(A, A)`, is NOT consistent with
% `g(int, string)` — the first occurrence commits `A` and the second
% must agree. Per-position independence used to accept exactly that,
% making repeated-variable bounds vacuously satisfiable.
sig_one_consistent(sig(DeclArgs, DeclRet), SubArgs, SubRet) ->
    is_mok(match_ty(DeclRet, SubRet, match_ty_list(DeclArgs, SubArgs, mok([])))).

% True when at least one declared signature in the list is consistent
% with the substituted bound. Implements the existence semantics of
% `check_bound`: any matching candidate discharges the bound, without
% committing to a particular candidate.
sig_existential([], _, _) -> false.
sig_existential([Sig|_], SubArgs, SubRet)
    | sig_one_consistent(Sig, SubArgs, SubRet) -> true.
sig_existential([_|Rest], SubArgs, SubRet) -> sig_existential(Rest, SubArgs, SubRet).

% True when no candidate signature is consistent with the substituted
% bound. Meaningful on a *partial* substitution too: a free variable
% in the substitution is consistent with anything (the matcher's var
% rules), so a ruled-out verdict cannot be un-ruled by later
% bindings — consistency only shrinks as information is added.
:- function (bound_ruled_out(list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(ty), ty) -> bool).
bound_ruled_out(Sigs, SubArgs, SubRet)
    | sig_existential(Sigs, SubArgs, SubRet) -> false.
bound_ruled_out(_, _, _) -> true.

% A *stable* witness: consistent with the substituted bound AND with
% no leaf that could still be bound (rigid counts as solid). Used
% for ambient candidates, whose variables may be shared with a
% still-flexible enclosing scope: an ambient that is only consistent
% because its own variable is unbound must not discharge the bound —
% a later binding could invalidate the witness with the check
% already gone, making accept/reject depend on body order.
:- function
    (sig_existential_stable(list(sig_t(list(ty), ty)), list(ty), ty) -> bool),
    (sig_stable_witness(sig_t(list(ty), ty), list(ty), ty) -> bool),
    (sig_ground(sig_t(list(ty), ty)) -> bool).

sig_existential_stable([], _, _) -> false.
sig_existential_stable([Sig|_], SubArgs, SubRet)
    | sig_stable_witness(Sig, SubArgs, SubRet) -> true.
sig_existential_stable([_|Rest], SubArgs, SubRet) ->
    sig_existential_stable(Rest, SubArgs, SubRet).

sig_stable_witness(Sig, SubArgs, SubRet)
    | sig_ground(Sig) -> sig_one_consistent(Sig, SubArgs, SubRet).
sig_stable_witness(_, _, _) -> false.

sig_ground(sig(Args, Ret)) | ty_concrete_list(Args) -> ty_concrete(Ret).
sig_ground(_) -> false.

% Ground-substitution check: true when every leaf of @SubArgs@ and
% @SubRet@ is a concrete type (no free type variable). When this is
% false the bound stays residual — per the gradual guarantee, a check
% that lacks the information to fail is not a failure.
:- function
    (sub_ground(list(ty), ty) -> bool),
    (ty_concrete(ty) -> bool),
    (ty_concrete_list(list(ty)) -> bool).

sub_ground(Args, Ret) | ty_concrete_list(Args) -> ty_concrete(Ret).
sub_ground(_, _) -> false.

ty_concrete(T) | var(T) -> false.
ty_concrete(ty_any) -> true.
ty_concrete(ty_int) -> true.
ty_concrete(ty_float) -> true.
ty_concrete(ty_string) -> true.
ty_concrete(tcon(_, Args)) -> ty_concrete_list(Args).
ty_concrete(fun(Args, Ret)) | ty_concrete_list(Args) -> ty_concrete(Ret).
% A rigid tvar is treated as concrete for the purpose of
% `sub_ground`. It is structurally ground (a fully-specified term)
% even though it stands for an abstract type — the gradual guarantee
% applies to /flexible/ unbound vars, not rigid identities. Without
% this, `check_bound` at a rigid σ would stay residual forever and
% its ambient-candidate discharge would never fire. A pinned rigid is
% as concrete as the type its cell holds.
ty_concrete(rigid(C, _)) | nonvar(C) -> ty_concrete(C).
ty_concrete(rigid(_, _)) -> true.
ty_concrete(_) -> false.

ty_concrete_list([]) -> true.
ty_concrete_list([T|Rest]) | ty_concrete(T) -> ty_concrete_list(Rest).
ty_concrete_list(_) -> false.

% ==========================================================================
% Declaration matching
% ==========================================================================

% Bounded constraint usage: copy_term the declaration packed with its
% bounds so the bound's logical variables stay shared with the head
% arguments' types. After unifying the argument list, emit one
% `check_bound` per bound at the freshly-renamed substitution. This
% rule is listed before `constraint_match` so the more-specific
% three-head pattern is preferred when both rules match.
bounded_constraint_match @
    constraint_sig(Name, Arity, DeclTypes), constraint_bounds(Name, Arity, Bounds) \
        check_constraint_use(Name, Arity, BoundCands, ArgTypes, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(sig(DeclTypes, Bounds))),
    FreshTypes is sig_fst(Fresh),
    FreshBounds is sig_snd(Fresh),
    check_arg_list(FreshTypes, ArgTypes, Ctx),
    emit_bounds(FreshBounds, BoundCands, Ctx).

% Constraint usage: copy_term the declaration, check args pairwise.
% `tc2_walk.chr` tells a `constraint_sig` for every constraint
% declared in the program (including untyped ones, which default to
% all-any), matching by name AND arity so arity-overloaded
% constraints each check against their own declaration, and the
% renamer rejects any rule referencing an undeclared constraint — so
% every check_constraint_use always has a matching constraint_sig.
constraint_match @
    constraint_sig(Name, Arity, DeclTypes) \
        check_constraint_use(Name, Arity, _, ArgTypes, Ctx) <=>
    FreshTypes is copy_term(quote(DeclTypes)),
    check_arg_list(FreshTypes, ArgTypes, Ctx).

% Bounded function usage: same shape as `function_match` plus a
% bound-discharge phase. Listed before `function_match` for the same
% reason `bounded_constraint_match` precedes `constraint_match`.
% Bounded functions are single-signature only (the spec restricts
% `requiring` to :- function / :- open_function, never :- class /
% :- open_class), so there is no bounded counterpart to
% `overloaded_function_match`.
% Every function fact and use-side constraint carries the arity, and
% the rule heads pair on (Name, Arity): functions can be
% arity-overloaded, and name-only pairing would let f/1's signature
% (or bounds) consume a use of f/2 — silently skipping the check, or
% attaching another arity's bounds to the wrong signature.
bounded_function_match @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, Sig), function_bounds(Name, Arity, Bounds) \
        check_function_use(Name, Arity, BoundCands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(sig(Sig, Bounds))),
    FreshSig is sig_fst(Fresh),
    FreshBounds is sig_snd(Fresh),
    FreshArgTypes is sig_fst(FreshSig),
    FreshRetType is sig_snd(FreshSig),
    check_arg_list(FreshArgTypes, ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(RetTypeVar, FreshRetType, Ctx),
    emit_bounds(FreshBounds, BoundCands, Ctx).

% Function usage: copy_term preserves arg/ret sharing
function_match @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, Sig) \
        check_function_use(Name, Arity, _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    FreshArgTypes is sig_fst(Fresh),
    FreshRetType is sig_snd(Fresh),
    check_arg_list(FreshArgTypes, ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(RetTypeVar, FreshRetType, Ctx).

% Overloaded function: hand the full candidate set to the residual
% resolution below. Multi-signature classes cannot carry `requiring`
% (RequiringOnClass), so declared overload candidates never have
% bounds. Unknown-function detection happens upstream: the resolver
% has already decided that a compound is a call rather than a
% constructor application, so this rule (and `function_match`) only
% fires for declared functions.
overloaded_function_match @
    function_sigs(Name, Arity, Sigs) \
        check_function_use(Name, Arity, _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    resolve_overload(Name, wrap_decls(Sigs), ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

% ==========================================================================
% Bounded polymorphism: ambient signatures at call sites
% ==========================================================================
%
% Inside the equations of a bounded function (or the body/guard of a
% rule whose head mentions a bounded constraint), calls to the bound's
% named functions see the bound's required signature(s) as additional
% candidates alongside the function's ordinary declared signatures.
% `tc2_walk.chr` determines which calls qualify and emits
% `check_function_use_with_ambient(Name, AmbSigs, BoundCands, Args,
% Ret, Ctx)` when the target name (at the call's arity) has at least
% one ambient signature in the surrounding scope. AmbSigs is the
% complete list of ambient signatures for that name; the walker
% computes it at call time — there is no CHR-side ambient store.

% With ambient + bounded single declared sig: the declared candidate
% carries its bounds, so a resolution that commits to the declaration
% discharges the callee's own bound at the resulting substitution
% (spec §Equation checking: use-site bound checks of bounded callees
% run under the enclosing ambient) — while a resolution that commits
% to an ambient candidate assumes the enclosing bound and emits none.
check_with_ambient_bounded @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, DeclSig), function_bounds(Name, Arity, Bounds) \
        check_function_use_with_ambient(Name, Arity, AmbSigs, BoundCands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Cands is append(wrap_ambs(AmbSigs), [decl_bounded(DeclSig, Bounds, BoundCands)]),
    resolve_overload(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

% With ambient + single declared sig: prepend ambients, treat as
% overload candidates.
check_with_ambient_single @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, DeclSig) \
        check_function_use_with_ambient(Name, Arity, AmbSigs, _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Cands is append(wrap_ambs(AmbSigs), [decl(DeclSig)]),
    resolve_overload(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

% With ambient + overloaded declared sigs.
check_with_ambient_multi @
    function_sigs(Name, Arity, DeclSigs) \
        check_function_use_with_ambient(Name, Arity, AmbSigs, _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Cands is append(wrap_ambs(AmbSigs), wrap_decls(DeclSigs)),
    resolve_overload(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

% No declared sig — the ambient sigs are the only candidates.
check_with_ambient_only @
    check_function_use_with_ambient(Name, _, AmbSigs, _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    resolve_overload(Name, wrap_ambs(AmbSigs), ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

% ==========================================================================
% Function references
% ==========================================================================
%
% `fun name/arity` is type-checked against the expected function type
% at its use site. Single-signature references keep unification
% semantics: instantiate the declared signature fresh and unify with
% the expected type (a mismatch is an ordinary inconsistency), plus
% the bound-discharge phase for a bounded referent. A reference to a
% multi-signature class is itself a residual overload resolution,
% filtered against the FULL expected type — return component
% included (spec §Function references: "a reference has no argument
% expressions, so the expected type is all the checker has").

% With ambient signatures in scope for the referenced name, even a
% single-signature reference goes through the residual resolution:
% inside a bounded scope, `fun g/1` must be able to resolve through
% the ambient g at the enclosing rigid tvars exactly like the call
% form does (mirrors check_with_ambient_bounded / _single).
funref_ambient_bounded @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, Sig), function_bounds(Name, Arity, Bounds) \
        check_function_ref(Name, Arity, [A | As], BoundCands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Cands is append(wrap_ambs([A | As]), [decl_bounded(Sig, Bounds, BoundCands)]),
    resolve_funref(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

funref_ambient_single @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, Sig) \
        check_function_ref(Name, Arity, [A | As], _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Cands is append(wrap_ambs([A | As]), [decl(Sig)]),
    resolve_funref(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

funref_bounded @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, Sig), function_bounds(Name, Arity, Bounds) \
        check_function_ref(Name, Arity, _, BoundCands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(sig(Sig, Bounds))),
    FreshSig is sig_fst(Fresh),
    FreshBounds is sig_snd(Fresh),
    check_arg_list(sig_fst(FreshSig), ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(RetTypeVar, sig_snd(FreshSig), Ctx),
    emit_bounds(FreshBounds, BoundCands, Ctx).

funref_single @
    function_sig(Name, Arity, Sig) \
        check_function_ref(Name, Arity, _, _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    check_arg_list(sig_fst(Fresh), ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(RetTypeVar, sig_snd(Fresh), Ctx).

funref_multi @
    function_sigs(Name, Arity, Sigs) \
        check_function_ref(Name, Arity, AmbSigs, _, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Cands is append(wrap_ambs(AmbSigs), wrap_decls(Sigs)),
    resolve_funref(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

% A reference at an arity with no declaration cannot match any rule
% above and stays residual; the renamer rejects such references
% upstream (YCHR-20002), so this is unreachable from valid programs.

% Same residual discipline as resolve_overload, with the return
% component participating in filtering.
resolve_funref_fire @
    resolve_funref(_, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
        candidates_committable(filter_consistent_full(Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar)) |
    commit_candidate(filter_consistent_full(Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar),
                     ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

resolve_funref_none @
    resolve_funref(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
        cand_list_empty(filter_consistent_full(Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar)) |
    report_error(Ctx, no_matching_overload, mangled_name(Name)).

% ==========================================================================
% Bounded polymorphism: bound emission and discharge
% ==========================================================================

% Walk a freshly-copy_term'd bounds list, emitting one residual
% `check_bound` constraint per entry. The bound's argument list and
% return type carry the call-site's fresh substitution (because the
% surrounding match rule copy_term'd them together with the function's
% signature), so the discharge rules see a substitution that is
% consistent with the call.
:- function
    (nbound_name(bound_named) -> any),
    (nbound_args(bound_named) -> list(ty)),
    (nbound_ret(bound_named) -> ty).
nbound_name(nbound(N, _, _)) -> N.
nbound_args(nbound(_, A, _)) -> A.
nbound_ret(nbound(_, _, R)) -> R.

emit_bounds_done @ emit_bounds([], _, _) <=> true.
emit_bounds_step @ emit_bounds([B | Rest], BoundCands, Ctx) <=>
    GName is nbound_name(B),
    BArgs is nbound_args(B),
    BRet is nbound_ret(B),
    check_bound(GName, bcand_ambs(GName, BoundCands),
                bcand_decls(GName, BoundCands), BArgs, BRet, Ctx),
    emit_bounds(Rest, BoundCands, Ctx).

% `check_bound` is a residual carrying its complete candidate set —
% the ambient signatures for the bound's named function in the
% enclosing scope plus that function's declared signatures, assembled
% by `tc2_walk.chr` at emission time (spec §Use-site checking step 4:
% "'declared signature' includes the ambient signatures contributed
% by the enclosing function's bound"). Ambient candidates share
% variable identity with the enclosing declaration's tvars by
% construction, which is what lets an equation-time bound check
% trivially satisfy itself under rigid type variables and keeps
% recursive uses of a bounded function polymorphic.
%
% Two dispositions (spec §Type Checking Procedure, residual checks):
%  - success needs a ground-enough substitution AND a *stable*
%    consistent witness: any declared candidate (its variables are
%    per-emission fresh and never bound, so the verdict cannot
%    change), or an ambient candidate whose own leaves are solid.
%    An ambient that is only consistent because it shares a
%    still-flexible variable with the enclosing scope leaves the
%    bound residual instead — a bound still residual at the end of
%    solving succeeds silently per the gradual guarantee;
%  - rule-out fires as soon as NO candidate is consistent — sound on
%    a partial substitution because consistency only shrinks as
%    information is added ("reports an error whenever that happens").
discharge_bound_ok_decl @
    check_bound(_, _, DeclSigs, SubArgs, SubRet, _) <=>
        sub_ground(SubArgs, SubRet),
        sig_existential(DeclSigs, SubArgs, SubRet) | true.

discharge_bound_ok_amb @
    check_bound(_, AmbSigs, _, SubArgs, SubRet, _) <=>
        sub_ground(SubArgs, SubRet),
        sig_existential_stable(AmbSigs, SubArgs, SubRet) | true.

discharge_bound_fail @
    check_bound(GName, AmbSigs, DeclSigs, SubArgs, SubRet, Ctx) <=>
        bound_ruled_out(append(AmbSigs, DeclSigs), SubArgs, SubRet) |
    report_error(Ctx, bound_unsatisfied, mangled_name(GName)).

% Constructor usage: copy_term preserves parent/field sharing
constructor_match @
    con_sig(ConName, Sig) \
        check_constructor_use(ConName, ArgTypes, ResultTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    FreshParent is sig_fst(Fresh),
    FreshFields is sig_snd(Fresh),
    check_arg_list(FreshFields, ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(ResultTypeVar, FreshParent, Ctx).

% Defensive fallback: unknown constructor. Every site in
% `tc2_walk.chr` that emits check_constructor_use — the two expression
% walks and the `guard_match` case — gates it on the constructor being
% a known declaration, so this rule is unreachable in practice. It is
% kept (and routed through tc_unify rather than raw `=`) so a future
% emitter that does not gate is still sound.
% Post no-bind semantics this is a pure consistency check: a flexible
% ResultTypeVar stays flexible (it is NOT widened to `any`).
unknown_constructor @
    check_constructor_use(_, _, ResultTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    tc_unify(ResultTypeVar, ty_any, Ctx).

% ==========================================================================
% Argument list checking
% ==========================================================================

check_arg_cons @
    check_arg_list([D|Ds], [A|As], Ctx) <=>
    tc_unify(A, D, Ctx),
    check_arg_list(Ds, As, Ctx).

check_arg_nil @
    check_arg_list([], [], _) <=> true.

% Note: mismatched list lengths (one side exhausted before the other)
% are left unsolved rather than reported as errors. The shape is
% unreachable from valid programs: constraint and function facts both
% match uses by name AND arity, constructors (which cannot be
% overloaded by arity) have their arity checked by `tc2_pure.chr`'s
% arity walk, and the renamer rejects wrong-arity calls and references
% upstream.

% ==========================================================================
% Delegation rules
% ==========================================================================

delegate_unify @
    check_unify(T1, T2, Ctx) <=> tc_unify(T1, T2, Ctx).

delegate_guard_bool @
    check_guard_bool(T, Ctx) <=>
    tc_unify(T, tcon(quote(prelude:bool), []), Ctx).

% --------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Constructor matches (HNF `GuardMatch`)
% --------------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% `check_guard_match(ScrutTy, Con, Betas, Slots, Ctx)` types one HNF
% `GuardMatch`. `Betas` holds one fresh RIGID type variable per
% parameter of `Con`'s parent type, and `Slots` one fresh slot per
% field of `Con`; both are allocated by the walker. The following
% `GuardGetArg`s read the field types straight out of `Slots`, so the
% scrutinee's type is consulted exactly once, here.
%
% A successful match is an evidence form: it proves the value was
% built by `Con`, and within the typed fragment `Con`-values inhabit
% only its parent type. What follows from that depends on the
% scrutinee's state (docs/reference/type-system.md §What evidence
% does):
%
%  - unpinned rigid: the match pins the skolem to the parent type at
%    the fresh rigid `Betas` — the matched value's field types are
%    store-chosen, so nothing downstream may assume more about them
%    than the match proved;
%  - pinned rigid: re-dispatch on the pinned type;
%  - `any`: inert on the scrutinee (nothing narrows `any`) while the
%    fields are typed at a fresh all-flexible instantiation;
%  - known type or flexible: the ordinary constructor-typing meet,
%    which is declaration-derived information rather than evidence —
%    a flexible scrutinee binds to the parent type, a known one has
%    its instantiation read off (matching `[X | Xs]` against
%    `list(T)` gives `X : T`, `Xs : list(T)`);
%  - known type that does not contain `Con`: constructor membership
%    fails, so the branch is inaccessible (YCHR-20104) and the fields
%    stay unconstrained.

:- function (tcon_args(ty) -> list(ty)).
tcon_args(tcon(_, Args)) -> Args.
% A data constructor's parent is always a type-constructor
% application, so the fallback is unreachable; it keeps the function
% total.
tcon_args(_) -> [].

guard_match_pinned @
    check_guard_match(rigid(C, _), Con, Betas, Slots, Ctx) <=> nonvar(C) |
    check_guard_match(C, Con, Betas, Slots, Ctx).

guard_match_rigid @
    con_sig(Con, Sig) \
        check_guard_match(rigid(C, _), Con, Betas, Slots, Ctx) <=> var(C) |
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    FreshParent is sig_fst(Fresh),
    tc_unify_list(tcon_args(FreshParent), Betas, Ctx),
    C = FreshParent,
    tc_unify_list(Slots, sig_snd(Fresh), Ctx).

guard_match_any @
    con_sig(Con, Sig) \
        check_guard_match(T, Con, _, Slots, Ctx) <=> nonvar(T), T == ty_any |
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    tc_unify_list(Slots, sig_snd(Fresh), Ctx).

guard_match_meet @
    con_sig(Con, Sig) \
        check_guard_match(T, Con, _, Slots, Ctx) <=>
        existential_consistent(sig_fst(Sig), T) |
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    tc_unify(T, sig_fst(Fresh), Ctx),
    tc_unify_list(Slots, sig_snd(Fresh), Ctx).

guard_match_inaccessible @
    con_sig(Con, Sig) \
        check_guard_match(T, Con, _, _, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    report_warning(Ctx, inaccessible, tpair(sig_fst(Fresh), T)).

% Defensive fallback: a constructor with no `con_sig`. `tc2_walk.chr`
% gates the emission on the constructor being declared (with a
% matching arity), so this is unreachable in practice; leaving the
% field slots unconstrained is the gradual disposition.
guard_match_unknown @
    check_guard_match(_, _, _, _, _) <=> true.

delegate_guard_getarg @
    con_sig(ConName, Sig) \
        check_guard_getarg(ResultType, TermType, ConName, FieldIndex, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    FreshParent is sig_fst(Fresh),
    FreshFields is sig_snd(Fresh),
    tc_unify(TermType, FreshParent, Ctx),
    FieldType is nth(FieldIndex, FreshFields),
    tc_unify(ResultType, FieldType, Ctx).

% Unknown constructor in guard getarg: nothing is known about the
% extracted field, so this is a no-op consistency check. Routed
% through tc_unify so the rule is sound when ResultType is already
% bound to a non-`any` type from an earlier constraint; a flexible
% ResultType stays flexible (the `any` here never binds).
unknown_guard_getarg @
    check_guard_getarg(ResultType, _, _, _, Ctx) <=>
    tc_unify(ResultType, ty_any, Ctx).

% ==========================================================================
% Occurs check (shared by tc_unify, ev_unify and eq_unify)
% ==========================================================================
%
% True when the variable `C` — a flexible slot, or a skolem's pin
% cell — occurs anywhere inside `T`. Compares by variable identity
% (ask-`==`, which is false for two distinct unbound variables) and
% looks through already-pinned cells, so an alias chain cannot hide
% an occurrence.
%
% Every relation below that BINDS a type variable guards its binding
% rules with this. A binding whose right-hand side contains the
% variable being bound has no finite solution and would build a
% cyclic term, which hangs the next deref that walks it — and the
% runtime's observer transfer walks the same terms, so a cyclic type
% term hangs the compiler outright rather than merely producing a
% strange type.

:- function
    (ty_occurs(ty, ty) -> bool),
    (ty_occurs_list(ty, list(ty)) -> bool).

ty_occurs(C, T) | var(T), C == T -> true.
ty_occurs(_, T) | var(T) -> false.
ty_occurs(C, rigid(C2, _)) | C == C2 -> true.
ty_occurs(C, rigid(C2, _)) | nonvar(C2) -> ty_occurs(C, C2).
ty_occurs(_, rigid(_, _)) -> false.
ty_occurs(C, tcon(_, Args)) -> ty_occurs_list(C, Args).
ty_occurs(C, fun(Args, _)) | ty_occurs_list(C, Args) -> true.
ty_occurs(C, fun(_, Ret)) -> ty_occurs(C, Ret).
ty_occurs(_, _) -> false.

ty_occurs_list(_, []) -> false.
ty_occurs_list(C, [T|_]) | ty_occurs(C, T) -> true.
ty_occurs_list(C, [_|Rest]) -> ty_occurs_list(C, Rest).

% ==========================================================================
% tc_unify (type propagation and consistency)
% ==========================================================================

% --- any handling (must come first) ---
%
% Per the meet table (type-system.md §Type states): `any` is only ever
% checked against — it never binds a type variable and never
% propagates. All three rules below therefore succeed WITHOUT binding
% anything. The invariant this preserves: a `ty` value equal to the
% atom `any` in a source-variable slot can only originate from the
% declaration-position stamping in `tc2_skel.chr` (`skeleton_of`,
% allocated by `alloc_var_types`). No CHR rule may bind a var to
% `any` — `tc2_walk.chr`'s `check_fun_stmt` relies on this to tell
% "declared any" apart from "still flexible", since it keeps a rebound
% local's `any`.

% (1) Nonvar any on left -> succeed, don't touch right side
tc_unify_any_left @
    tc_unify(T1, _, _) <=> nonvar(T1), T1 == ty_any | true.

% (2) Both nonvar, any on right -> succeed
tc_unify_any_right @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, _) <=> nonvar(T1), nonvar(T2), T2 == ty_any | true.

% (3) Var on left, any on right -> succeed, do NOT bind. The flexible
% variable stays flexible: a later solid source may still bind it,
% and if none does it simply remains unconstrained (spec: "flexible
% α, any -> succeeds; α is not bound").
tc_unify_var_any @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, _) <=> var(T1), nonvar(T2), T2 == ty_any | true.

% --- base types ---

tc_unify_int @
    tc_unify(ty_int, ty_int, _) <=> true.

tc_unify_float @
    tc_unify(ty_float, ty_float, _) <=> true.

tc_unify_string @
    tc_unify(ty_string, ty_string, _) <=> true.

% --- type constructors and function types: same shape, check args ---
%
% [C-Con] and [C-Fun] require the same argument count on both sides.
% The guarded rules fire only when the argument lists have equal
% length and then check the component types pairwise; the unguarded
% fallbacks fire on an arity mismatch and report the two whole types
% as inconsistent. Without the arity guard a mismatch would reach
% `tc_unify_list` with lists of unequal length, which matches no
% `tc_unify_list` rule and silently stays an inert residual. (For
% tcon the mismatch is defensive: the renamer resolves type names by
% name+arity, so a well-formed program never produces two same-named
% tcons of different arity — but declaration-validation gaps can.)

:- function (same_arity(list(ty), list(ty)) -> bool).

same_arity([], []) -> true.
same_arity([_|Xs], [_|Ys]) -> same_arity(Xs, Ys).
same_arity(_, _) -> false.

tc_unify_tcon @
    tc_unify(tcon(C, Args1), tcon(C, Args2), Ctx) <=>
    same_arity(Args1, Args2) |
    tc_unify_list(Args1, Args2, Ctx).

tc_unify_tcon_arity @
    tc_unify(tcon(C, Args1), tcon(C, Args2), Ctx) <=>
    report_error(Ctx, inconsistent, tpair(tcon(C, Args1), tcon(C, Args2))).

tc_unify_fun @
    tc_unify(fun(A1, R1), fun(A2, R2), Ctx) <=>
    same_arity(A1, A2) |
    tc_unify_list(A1, A2, Ctx),
    tc_unify(R1, R2, Ctx).

tc_unify_fun_arity @
    tc_unify(fun(A1, R1), fun(A2, R2), Ctx) <=>
    report_error(Ctx, inconsistent, tpair(fun(A1, R1), fun(A2, R2))).

% --- rigid type variables ---
%
% Same-rigid: succeed silently. Different-rigid or rigid-vs-concrete
% falls through to tc_unify_error and reports an inconsistency. A
% flexible var meeting a rigid is handled by the var rules below
% (the flex side binds to the rigid term).
%
% A *pinned* rigid — one whose cell guard-derived evidence has bound
% — is not opaque any more: the ordinary meet sees the type the cell
% holds. This is how an evidence guard enables an operation the
% skolem would otherwise reject (`show(X) | integer(X) -> ...`).
% Pinning is the only sanctioned exception to the meet table's rigid
% rows and only `ev_unify` performs it.

tc_unify_rigid @
    tc_unify(rigid(C, N), rigid(C, N), _) <=> true.

tc_unify_rigid_pinned_left @
    tc_unify(rigid(C, _), T2, Ctx) <=> nonvar(C) | tc_unify(C, T2, Ctx).

tc_unify_rigid_pinned_right @
    tc_unify(T1, rigid(C, _), Ctx) <=> nonvar(C) | tc_unify(T1, C, Ctx).

% --- var rules ---
%
% Occurs check first, with the same accepting disposition `eq_unify`
% uses: a slot that would be bound to a type containing itself has no
% finite solution, so the fact is dropped and nothing is reported.
% The slot is flexible, which means the values reaching it are typed
% `any` somewhere upstream — rejecting here would move the checker in
% the rejecting direction on unannotated code. The point is to not
% build the cyclic term; see the occurs-check block above.

tc_unify_occurs_left @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, _) <=> var(T1), nonvar(T2), ty_occurs(T1, T2) | true.

tc_unify_occurs_right @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, _) <=> nonvar(T1), var(T2), ty_occurs(T2, T1) | true.

tc_unify_var_nonvar @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, _) <=> var(T1), nonvar(T2) | T1 = T2.

tc_unify_nonvar_var @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, _) <=> nonvar(T1), var(T2) | T2 = T1.

tc_unify_var_var @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, _) <=> var(T1), var(T2) | T1 = T2.

% --- fallback: inconsistency ---

tc_unify_error @
    tc_unify(T1, T2, Ctx) <=> nonvar(T1), nonvar(T2) |
    report_error(Ctx, inconsistent, tpair(T1, T2)).

% ==========================================================================
% tc_unify_list
% ==========================================================================

tc_unify_list_nil @
    tc_unify_list([], [], _) <=> true.

tc_unify_list_cons @
    tc_unify_list([H1|T1], [H2|T2], Ctx) <=>
    tc_unify(H1, H2, Ctx),
    tc_unify_list(T1, T2, Ctx).

% ==========================================================================
% Fresh rigid supply
% ==========================================================================
%
% A gensym for rigid type variables allocated from *inside* the
% rules. Declaration-site skolems get ids 0, 1, 2, ... — from
% `decl_rigid_supply` in `tc2_walk.chr` — and this supply counts DOWN
% from -1 so the two ranges can never collide (rigid identity
% comparisons look at the cell and the id, and negative ids surface
% only cosmetically, as `T#-1` in messages). `tc2_main.chr` seeds
% `rigid_supply(-1)` once per check.
%
% Both ranges are user-visible — an unpinned skolem renders as `T#n`
% — so the ids and the *order* they are handed out in are part of what
% a diagnostic says. `sort_unique_tvars` and `make_decl_rigid` in
% `tc2_walk.chr` fix the declaration-site order;
% `test/golden/typecheck_tvar_order/` covers it.
%
% The pin cell of the freshly built skolem is `Cell`, a body-only
% variable — which is exactly a fresh unbound logical variable, so the
% pin rules can fill it in later.

make_fresh_rigid1 @
    rigid_supply(N), make_fresh_rigid(Out) <=>
    N1 is N - 1,
    Out = rigid(Cell, N),
    rigid_supply(N1).

% One fresh rigid per element of the input list (only the length is
% used; the elements are never inspected).
make_fresh_rigids_nil @
    make_fresh_rigids([], Out) <=> Out = [].

make_fresh_rigids_cons @
    make_fresh_rigids([_ | Rest], Out) <=>
    make_fresh_rigid(B),
    make_fresh_rigids(Rest, Tail),
    Out = [B | Tail].

% True for the base types, which are fully determined by their
% values: every `int` value is an `int` and nothing else. Used by the
% top-level pin rules; `ty_any` is deliberately not included (`any`
% is disposed of before pinning) and neither are `tcon`/`fun`/`rigid`
% (each has its own pin discipline).
:- function (base_type(ty) -> bool).

base_type(ty_int) -> true.
base_type(ty_float) -> true.
base_type(ty_string) -> true.
base_type(_) -> false.

% ==========================================================================
% ev_unify (guard-derived type evidence)
% ==========================================================================
%
% The evidence meet of docs/reference/type-system.md §Guard-Derived
% Type Evidence §What evidence does. `ev_unify(FactTy, SlotTy, D, Ctx)`
% asserts the typing fact a guard's operational success entails: the
% fact the guard establishes comes first, the type the position
% already has second (the order the YCHR-20104 message reads them in;
% for a symmetric form like `GuardEqual` either order is meaningful).
% `D` is the `ev_depth` of the pair: `ev_top` for two whole types,
% `ev_param` once descent has entered a shared type constructor.
% It differs from `tc_unify` in both directions:
%
%  - it is the ONLY thing that may bind a rigid tvar's pin cell,
%    which is what lets a guard enable an operation the skolem would
%    otherwise reject. What the pin may say is bounded by what value
%    equality proves: a value determines its type's outermost
%    constructor nominally but not the constructor's parameters
%    (`[]` inhabits `list(tau)` for every `tau`), so at `ev_top` a
%    skolem pins to a base type or nullary constructor application
%    exactly, to a parametric application or function type only at
%    FRESH rigid parameters (the guard_match_rigid discipline), and
%    merges with another skolem; at `ev_param` evidence derives
%    nothing about rigid cells at all — a parameter-erasing value
%    may account for the equality;
%  - it never binds a flexible variable and never narrows `any` —
%    at those states evidence is inert, so it cannot restrict
%    unannotated code (the asymmetry is the design principle: at a
%    rigid variable evidence is purely enabling, elsewhere it could
%    only reject);
%  - it is a *residual* check, like `resolve_overload` and
%    `check_bound`: while either side is still an unsolved variable
%    no rule below fires, so the constraint stays in the store and is
%    re-tried when solving binds it. Evidence acts on the type's
%    state per the spec's model — "solve the gathered constraints,
%    then dispatch on the state" — which is what keeps the verdict
%    independent of the order constraints were gathered in. A fact
%    still pending when solving ends was inert: its slot never became
%    anything but flexible;
%  - a contradiction between two whole types is a *warning*, not an
%    error: the guard can never succeed within the typed fragment,
%    so the enclosing rule or equation is dead code (YCHR-20104).
%    The contradicting fact binds nothing and checking continues
%    with the declared types. A mismatch confined to `ev_param`
%    positions is NOT a contradiction — `box(int)` and `box(bool)`
%    positions can share the value `empty` — and derives nothing.

ev_unify_any_left @
    ev_unify(T1, _, _, _) <=> nonvar(T1), T1 == ty_any | true.

ev_unify_any_right @
    ev_unify(_, T2, _, _) <=> nonvar(T2), T2 == ty_any | true.

ev_unify_same_rigid @
    ev_unify(rigid(C, N), rigid(C, N), _, _) <=> true.

% Parameter depth: an unpinned skolem on either side means the fact
% is vacuous — drop it. (A *pinned* skolem is no longer opaque and
% falls through to the deref rules below.)
ev_unify_param_rigid_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C, _), _, ev_param, _) <=> var(C) | true.

ev_unify_param_rigid_right @
    ev_unify(_, rigid(C, _), ev_param, _) <=> var(C) | true.

% Top level, rigid ~ rigid: merge the two skolems by pinning the
% unpinned cell to the other side's term (the multi-head idiom of a
% polymorphic constraint). These fire ahead of the pinned-deref rules
% so a merge reaches THROUGH a pin: "pin then merge" and "merge then
% pin" leave the same shared structure. The alias guards stop a
% cyclic merge — `ty_occurs` looks through pinned cells, so a skolem
% already reachable from the other side's alias/pin chain is the same
% skolem and the fact is spent.
ev_unify_rigid_alias_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C1, _), rigid(C2, N2), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C1), ty_occurs(C1, rigid(C2, N2)) | true.

ev_unify_rigid_alias_right @
    ev_unify(rigid(C1, N1), rigid(C2, _), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C2), ty_occurs(C2, rigid(C1, N1)) | true.

ev_unify_rigid_merge_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C1, _), rigid(C2, N2), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C1) | C1 = rigid(C2, N2).

ev_unify_rigid_merge_right @
    ev_unify(rigid(C1, N1), rigid(C2, _), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C2) | C2 = rigid(C1, N1).

% A pinned skolem is no longer opaque: the fact meets whatever the
% cell holds (so a second, contradicting predicate on the same
% variable reports 20104 rather than silently re-pinning).
ev_unify_pinned_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C, _), T2, D, Ctx) <=> nonvar(C) | ev_unify(C, T2, D, Ctx).

ev_unify_pinned_right @
    ev_unify(T1, rigid(C, _), D, Ctx) <=> nonvar(C) | ev_unify(T1, C, D, Ctx).

% Top-level pinning, by the shape of the solid partner. A base type
% or a nullary constructor application is determined by its values,
% so the pin copies it exactly. A still-flexible partner leaves the
% fact residual (no rule fires), and `any` was disposed of already.
ev_unify_pin_base_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C, _), T2, ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C), nonvar(T2), base_type(T2) | C = T2.

ev_unify_pin_base_right @
    ev_unify(T1, rigid(C, _), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C), nonvar(T1), base_type(T1) | C = T1.

ev_unify_pin_nullary_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C, _), tcon(Ctor, []), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C) | C = tcon(Ctor, []).

ev_unify_pin_nullary_right @
    ev_unify(tcon(Ctor, []), rigid(C, _), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C) | C = tcon(Ctor, []).

% A parametric application or function type is NOT determined by its
% values, so the pin commits to the outermost constructor only, at
% fresh rigid parameters unrelated to the partner's (the same
% discipline guard_match_rigid applies at a rigid scrutinee). No
% descent into the partner follows the pin: it would run at
% `ev_param`, where nothing is derived, so it is a no-op by
% construction. Every pinned value is rigid-free or built from fresh
% skolems, so no occurs check is needed on these rules.
ev_unify_pin_parametric_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C, _), tcon(Ctor, [A | As]), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C) |
    make_fresh_rigids([A | As], Betas),
    C = tcon(Ctor, Betas).

ev_unify_pin_parametric_right @
    ev_unify(tcon(Ctor, [A | As]), rigid(C, _), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C) |
    make_fresh_rigids([A | As], Betas),
    C = tcon(Ctor, Betas).

ev_unify_pin_fun_left @
    ev_unify(rigid(C, _), fun(Args, _), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C) |
    make_fresh_rigids(Args, ArgBetas),
    make_fresh_rigid(RetBeta),
    C = fun(ArgBetas, RetBeta).

ev_unify_pin_fun_right @
    ev_unify(fun(Args, _), rigid(C, _), ev_top, _) <=>
    var(C) |
    make_fresh_rigids(Args, ArgBetas),
    make_fresh_rigid(RetBeta),
    C = fun(ArgBetas, RetBeta).

ev_unify_int @
    ev_unify(ty_int, ty_int, _, _) <=> true.

ev_unify_float @
    ev_unify(ty_float, ty_float, _, _) <=> true.

ev_unify_string @
    ev_unify(ty_string, ty_string, _, _) <=> true.

% Descent into a shared constructor switches to `ev_param`: the
% component facts are about parameter positions, where equality
% evidence is vacuous for rigid cells and a mismatch is not a
% contradiction.
ev_unify_tcon @
    ev_unify(tcon(C, Args1), tcon(C, Args2), _, Ctx) <=>
    same_arity(Args1, Args2) |
    ev_unify_list(Args1, Args2, ev_param, Ctx).

ev_unify_fun @
    ev_unify(fun(A1, R1), fun(A2, R2), _, Ctx) <=>
    same_arity(A1, A2) |
    ev_unify_list(A1, A2, ev_param, Ctx),
    ev_unify(R1, R2, ev_param, Ctx).

ev_unify_contradiction @
    ev_unify(T1, T2, ev_top, Ctx) <=> nonvar(T1), nonvar(T2) |
    report_warning(Ctx, inaccessible, tpair(T1, T2)).

% Parameter-position mismatch: satisfiable via a parameter-erasing
% value, so it neither warns nor binds.
ev_unify_param_mismatch @
    ev_unify(T1, T2, ev_param, _) <=> nonvar(T1), nonvar(T2) | true.

ev_unify_list_nil @
    ev_unify_list([], [], _, _) <=> true.

ev_unify_list_cons @
    ev_unify_list([H1|T1], [H2|T2], D, Ctx) <=>
    ev_unify(H1, H2, D, Ctx),
    ev_unify_list(T1, T2, D, Ctx).

% ==========================================================================
% eq_unify (the HNF equality meet)
% ==========================================================================
%
% What a `GuardEqual` asserts. HNF emits it in two situations, and it
% carries both readings at once:
%
%  - a source variable repeated across head (or parameter) positions,
%    split by HNF into two variables — so the two type slots belong
%    to ONE source variable, and §Type states' "a variable with a
%    concrete or type-parameter source keeps that source, and the
%    `any` positions are merely checked" applies across the split;
%  - a non-variable head argument (`p(0)`), where the match against
%    the literal is a genuine run-time fact.
%
% Accordingly `eq_unify` binds like `tc_unify` where a slot is still
% flexible — that is the declaration-source merge, not evidence, and
% it neither restricts unannotated code (an `any`-stamped slot is
% disposed of first) nor reports anything — and delegates to
% `ev_unify` where both sides are solid, which is where the evidence
% reading matters: two skolems merge (the multi-head idiom of a
% polymorphic constraint), a literal pins one, and a contradiction
% between known types marks the rule dead (YCHR-20104) rather than
% inconsistent.
%
% The flexible merge has to reach INSIDE type constructors, because a
% slot's two halves are routinely structured: matching `p([E | X])`
% at an untyped `p` types X as `list(alpha)` with alpha flexible, and
% a partner `q(X)` at `q(list(int))` is what says alpha is int. So
% `eq_unify` recurses through `tcon`/`fun` itself and hands only
% LEAVES to `ev_unify` — delegating the whole structure would lose
% the merge at every flexible component, since evidence is
% deliberately inert there. The descent switches the depth to
% `ev_param`, which changes nothing for flexibles (they bind at every
% depth) but makes the delegated rigid leaves vacuous: value equality
% does not determine a shared constructor's parameters, so evidence
% derives nothing about rigid cells there (`ev_unify`'s block
% comment). Every case is disposed of here or in `ev_unify`'s solid
% rules, so an `eq_unify` never stays residual (an `ev_unify` it
% delegates to may, which is that relation's documented behaviour).
%
% OPEN QUESTION (inference axis, not evidence): the flexible binding
% at `ev_param` is itself justified only by the declaration-source
% reading — the two halves are one variable. Where a half is
% flexible-inside-a-constructor and the other half's parameter is
% concrete, the bound type may over-commit for a parameter-erasing
% witness (the same argument that makes rigid parameter pins
% unsound). No unsoundness witness through a flexible has been
% found; revisit if the soundness property test produces one.

eq_unify_any_left @
    eq_unify(T1, _, _, _) <=> nonvar(T1), T1 == ty_any | true.

eq_unify_any_right @
    eq_unify(_, T2, _, _) <=> nonvar(T2), T2 == ty_any | true.

% Occurs check on the binding rules, ahead of them. `p([X | X])`
% equates alpha with `list(alpha)`; binding would build a cyclic term
% and hang every later deref. It reports NOTHING: the slot is
% flexible, so the values reaching it are typed `any` somewhere
% upstream, and an improper list like `[1 | 1]` really can match the
% pattern at run time. Dropping the fact is the accepting disposition
% — the checker does not move in the rejecting direction on
% unannotated code. (The rigid analogue needs no occurs check at all:
% a skolem pin never copies the other side's structure beyond the
% outermost constructor, so no cycle can be built — `T` against
% `list(T)` pins `T := list(beta)` with a fresh `beta`.)
eq_unify_occurs_left @
    eq_unify(T1, T2, _, _) <=> var(T1), nonvar(T2), ty_occurs(T1, T2) | true.

eq_unify_occurs_right @
    eq_unify(T1, T2, _, _) <=> nonvar(T1), var(T2), ty_occurs(T2, T1) | true.

eq_unify_var_nonvar @
    eq_unify(T1, T2, _, _) <=> var(T1), nonvar(T2) | T1 = T2.

eq_unify_nonvar_var @
    eq_unify(T1, T2, _, _) <=> nonvar(T1), var(T2) | T2 = T1.

eq_unify_var_var @
    eq_unify(T1, T2, _, _) <=> var(T1), var(T2) | T1 = T2.

% A whole rigid ~ rigid pair delegates with both wrappers intact,
% ahead of the pinned-deref rules: `ev_unify`'s merge rules reach
% through a pin, so "pin then merge" and "merge then pin" leave the
% same shared structure. Dereferencing first would instead re-pin the
% unpinned side at fresh parameters, losing the sharing.
eq_unify_rigid_pair @
    eq_unify(rigid(C1, N1), rigid(C2, N2), D, Ctx) <=>
    ev_unify(rigid(C1, N1), rigid(C2, N2), D, Ctx).

% Below the binding rules, so that a flexible slot meeting a skolem
% still binds to the skolem term itself (rigidity travels through
% flexibles) rather than to what the skolem is pinned to.
eq_unify_pinned_left @
    eq_unify(rigid(C, _), T2, D, Ctx) <=> nonvar(C) | eq_unify(C, T2, D, Ctx).

eq_unify_pinned_right @
    eq_unify(T1, rigid(C, _), D, Ctx) <=> nonvar(C) | eq_unify(T1, C, D, Ctx).

% Descent into a shared constructor switches to `ev_param` (see the
% block comment).
eq_unify_tcon @
    eq_unify(tcon(C, Args1), tcon(C, Args2), _, Ctx) <=>
    same_arity(Args1, Args2) |
    eq_unify_list(Args1, Args2, ev_param, Ctx).

eq_unify_fun @
    eq_unify(fun(A1, R1), fun(A2, R2), _, Ctx) <=>
    same_arity(A1, A2) |
    eq_unify_list(A1, A2, ev_param, Ctx),
    eq_unify(R1, R2, ev_param, Ctx).

% Leaves, plus every mismatch: an unpinned skolem on either side, two
% base types, a tcon meeting a fun, a same-name tcon at two arities.
eq_unify_evidence @
    eq_unify(T1, T2, D, Ctx) <=> ev_unify(T1, T2, D, Ctx).

eq_unify_list_nil @
    eq_unify_list([], [], _, _) <=> true.

eq_unify_list_cons @
    eq_unify_list([H1|T1], [H2|T2], D, Ctx) <=>
    eq_unify(H1, H2, D, Ctx),
    eq_unify_list(T1, T2, D, Ctx).

% ==========================================================================
% Overload resolution
% ==========================================================================
%
% Resolution is a residual check (spec §Type Checking Procedure):
% `resolve_overload` keeps the FULL candidate list and stays in the
% store, re-filtering on every activation as solving adds
% information (the runtime reactivates it whenever a variable
% reachable from its arguments is bound; when a binding makes new
% variables reachable — X := f(A) — the runtime transfers X's
% observers onto them, so multi-step chains re-trigger too).
%
%  - It FIRES — commits to the surviving signature and propagates —
%    only when all surviving candidates are equal after substitution
%    (`candidates_committable`); firing may enable other pending
%    checks, which the reactivation queue re-runs.
%  - An empty surviving set reports `no_matching_overload` whenever
%    it occurs (sound eagerly: with only flexible variables in play,
%    consistency shrinks monotonically as information is added).
%  - A resolution that never fires simply stays in the store: that
%    IS the "still ambiguous when solving ends succeeds silently"
%    disposition — its result type stays flexible and nothing is
%    propagated.
%  - A fired commitment is never retracted; if later information
%    contradicts it, the contradiction surfaces as an ordinary
%    inconsistency from the committed `check_arg_list`/`tc_unify`.

resolve_fire @
    resolve_overload(_, Cands, ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
        candidates_committable(filter_consistent(Cands, ArgTypes)) |
    commit_candidate(filter_consistent(Cands, ArgTypes),
                     ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx).

resolve_none @
    resolve_overload(Name, Cands, ArgTypes, _, Ctx) <=>
        no_candidate_consistent(Cands, ArgTypes) |
    report_error(Ctx, no_matching_overload, mangled_name(Name)).

% Committing applies the first surviving candidate's signature per
% its provenance: ambient signatures in place (identity sharing with
% the enclosing declaration's tvars is the point), declared
% signatures freshly renamed, bounded declarations copied together
% with their bounds so the emitted bound checks carry the call site's
% substitution. The head patterns match the non-empty list shape;
% `resolve_fire`'s guard guarantees it.
commit_amb @
    commit_candidate([amb(Sig) | _], ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    check_arg_list(sig_fst(Sig), ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(RetTypeVar, sig_snd(Sig), Ctx).

commit_decl @
    commit_candidate([decl(Sig) | _], ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Sig)),
    check_arg_list(sig_fst(Fresh), ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(RetTypeVar, sig_snd(Fresh), Ctx).

commit_decl_bounded @
    commit_candidate([decl_bounded(Sig, Bounds, BoundCands) | _], ArgTypes, RetTypeVar, Ctx) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(sig(Sig, Bounds))),
    FreshSig is sig_fst(Fresh),
    FreshBounds is sig_snd(Fresh),
    check_arg_list(sig_fst(FreshSig), ArgTypes, Ctx),
    tc_unify(RetTypeVar, sig_snd(FreshSig), Ctx),
    emit_bounds(FreshBounds, BoundCands, Ctx).
